<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolesław II of Masovia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1251–<a href="/years/1313">1313</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Boleslaw II of Masovia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolesław II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> was a Piast duke who received Płock when the Duchy of Masovia was divided in 1275, and later reunited most of Masovia under his rule. He was briefly Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> in 1288 and was married to a Lithuanian princess, which tied Masovia to its pagan neighbour. On his death his lands were again divided among his sons.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bolesław was the younger son of Siemowit I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> and Pereyaslava, a Rus' princess whom some historians make a daughter of Prince Daniel of Halych and others a distant relative of his. Little is known of his early years, except that he was not with his father and his elder brother Konrad II at Jazdów in 1262, when Siemowit was killed and Konrad captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>. His mother and his father's ally Bolesław the Pious, Duke of Greater Poland, governed as regents until Konrad was freed in 1264.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Coming of age in 1275, Bolesław demanded his share of the inheritance and received western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> with Płock as its capital. Dissatisfied, he fought Konrad for a decade, calling on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> who had killed his father; in 1279 he married Gaudemantė, daughter of Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traidenis">Traidenis</a>, baptised Sophia. The war brought neither side gain and laid waste much of Masovia.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-black">Leszek II the Black</a> died childless on 30 September 1288, Bishop Paweł of Przemankowo and the Topór family backed Bolesław for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. His troops took Kraków and Sandomierz in 1288, but Henryk IV Probus drove him out after the castellan Sulk the Bear opened Wawel Castle. On 26 February 1289 Bolesław's allies won the Battle of Siewierz, and he entered Kraków that summer, yet when he handed Sandomierz to Konrad the nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> rejected him, and he withdrew to Płock.</p><p>In 1291 he married Kunigunde, sister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a>. When Konrad died without a son in 1294, Bolesław became ruler of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and returned to Władysław I the Elbow-high. Wenceslaus attacked Masovia and destroyed Płock, but Bolesław refused to recognise him as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1300 and sent Kunigunde back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1302.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the early 1300s Bolesław founded the stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, which grew at the expense of nearby Czersk. In 1310 he gave Warsaw to his son Siemowit II and Czersk to Trojden I. He died on 20 April 1313 at Wyszogród and was buried in Płock Cathedral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bolesław II of Masovia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1275 the Duchy of Masovia was split, and the part centred on Plock, an early Piast capital and bishopric on the Vistula, went to <b>Boleslaw II of Masovia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-plock">Duchy of Plock</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Boleslaw II of Masovia</b> died in 1313, his lands were divided among his sons, and Siemowit II received the southern part around Rawa.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-rawa">Duchy of Rawa</a></li></ul></section>
